"""Original environmental-health drill; the first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
Environmental health concerns|relationships between surroundings and health|only the score in a game|only inherited characteristics|one person's name|Environmental health considers how surroundings and conditions influence health.
A hazard is|something with potential to cause harm|a guarantee that harm has occurred|a complete medical diagnosis|always a visible object|A hazard has the potential to cause harm.
Exposure means|contact with a hazard|proof of illness in every case|the same as a medical diagnosis|a competition format|Exposure describes contact with a hazard.
Risk concerns|the likelihood of harm in a particular context|only the name of a hazard|the certainty of harm in every case|the number of people in a team|Risk concerns the likelihood of harm under particular circumstances.
Accumulated smoke in a poorly ventilated room|can expose occupants to harmful air pollution|is always harmless|guarantees good respiratory health|replaces the need for ventilation|Smoke can contain harmful pollutants and poor ventilation can increase exposure.
Improved ventilation and cleaner energy where feasible|can reduce some exposure to cooking smoke|guarantee zero risk in every setting|eliminate the need to consider affordability|are identical in availability everywhere|Exposure reduction depends on conditions, resources and the intervention.
Health advice is more useful when it considers|local resources and practical barriers|only the speaker's preference|the assumption that all facilities are available|a guarantee that every plan will work identically|Practical feasibility and access influence whether advice can be followed.
Clear-looking water|can still contain harmful microorganisms or chemicals|is guaranteed safe to drink|never needs treatment|cannot be contaminated during storage|Many water contaminants cannot be detected by appearance alone.
Safe water management can include|source protection, suitable treatment and safe storage|only checking colour|storing water in any container regardless of cleanliness|ignoring the source|Safe water requires attention to the source, treatment and prevention of recontamination.
Boiling water correctly can|kill many disease-causing organisms|remove every toxic chemical|guarantee all sources are chemically safe|replace every local advisory|Boiling can kill many pathogens but does not remove all chemical pollutants.
If water may contain fuel or toxic chemicals, boiling|does not make it safe from those chemicals|always removes them|turns them into nutrients|proves the source is safe|Boiling does not make fuel- or chemically contaminated water safe; follow local guidance and use a safe source.
Safe storage after treatment helps|reduce recontamination|guarantee that every source is safe|replace all water treatment|identify all chemical pollutants by sight|Clean, covered storage can help prevent recontamination.
Sanitation includes|safe management of human excreta|only the colour of toilet walls|only making a poster|only the number of windows|Sanitation involves safe management of excreta, not just construction.
A toilet is most useful when it is|accessible, functional and maintained|built but unusable|unmaintained and unsafe|counted without checking use|Function, access and maintenance are important to sanitation services.
The sanitation chain includes|containment and safe treatment or disposal|only a building's paint|only a health slogan|only counting users|Safe sanitation considers where excreta goes and how it is managed.
Handwashing after toilet use can|help interrupt transmission of infection|guarantee no illness ever occurs|replace all sanitation facilities|make contaminated water safe by itself|Handwashing helps reduce transmission but is one part of a wider system.
A school lacking soap and water faces|a practical barrier to hand hygiene|a guarantee of safe practice|evidence that facilities do not matter|proof that all pupils have identical resources|Without supplies, learners may be unable to follow hygiene advice consistently.
Waste management can include|reduction, separation, collection, treatment and safe disposal|only leaving waste uncollected|burning every type of waste without safeguards|ignoring where waste goes|Waste management is a process involving several stages.
Discarded needles should|be handled through appropriate trained services, not with bare hands|be picked up with bare hands|be placed in a child's play area|be treated as harmless|Sharps can cause injury and infection risk and need special handling.
Open dumping of waste can|create injury and environmental contamination risks|guarantee safe water|replace collection services|eliminate vectors in every case|Open dumping can contribute to hazards and contamination.
Burning waste can|expose people to harmful smoke|guarantee clean air|make every material safe|replace all treatment requirements|Waste burning can produce harmful air pollution.
A vector is|a living organism that can transmit a disease-causing agent between hosts|the disease-causing agent in every case|a complete diagnosis|a synonym for safe water|A vector can transmit an agent, but is not necessarily the pathogen itself.
Which agent causes malaria?|Plasmodium parasites|all mosquitoes as organisms|unsafe water alone|a vitamin deficiency|Malaria is caused by Plasmodium parasites.
Malaria is commonly transmitted by|infected female Anopheles mosquitoes|every insect bite|sharing food in ordinary social contact|a clean handwashing station|Infected female Anopheles mosquitoes are common malaria vectors.
An insecticide-treated net used appropriately can|reduce mosquito bites and malaria risk|guarantee that no infection can occur|make unsafe drinking water safe|replace all other recommended prevention|Treated nets are one important measure in malaria prevention.
Removing standing water can|reduce breeding sites for some mosquitoes|guarantee malaria elimination by itself|replace appropriate nets and local control measures|make all mosquitoes disappear immediately|Reducing standing water can help, but is not a complete prevention package.
A community health programme should begin by|identifying the problem and listening to affected people|assuming every community has identical needs|ignoring local knowledge|deciding the outcome without collecting information|Understanding the problem and involving the community supports relevant planning.
A school programme installs handwashing stations. A useful follow-up is to check|whether water and soap remain available and stations function|only the colours of the walls|only the date a poster was printed|whether all pupils have identical names|Function and maintained supplies matter to actual access.
Counting facilities alone|does not establish whether they are functional or accessible|proves the programme succeeded in every respect|replaces all evaluation|shows that no maintenance is needed|A count does not reveal the quality, accessibility or use of facilities.
Health communication should be|clear, respectful and accessible to the intended audience|based on blame for every barrier|inaccessible to the affected community|a substitute for all facilities|Clear and inclusive communication helps people understand and use information.
Safe waste management is|a shared responsibility of individuals, services and authorities|only an individual responsibility with no role for services|only a slogan|guaranteed by a single poster|Individuals and institutions have complementary responsibilities.
An environmental hazard and exposure differ because|one has potential to harm and the other is contact with it|they always mean a diagnosis|exposure is always a guarantee of disease|a hazard is always harmless|Hazard refers to potential harm; exposure refers to contact.
A risk assessment should consider|the nature and duration of exposure and context|only the name of the hazard|only a person's initials|the assumption that all exposures are identical|Context and exposure characteristics affect the likelihood of harm.
Unsafe water and inadequate sanitation can|contribute to the spread of disease|guarantee that infections will not occur|replace hygiene measures|affect only sports results|Unsafe water and sanitation can contribute to transmission of infections.
Access to water and sanitation is influenced by|infrastructure, resources and maintenance|individual knowledge alone in every case|only a team's colour|only the weather on one day|Resources and infrastructure affect whether facilities are available and usable.
Which action helps reduce cross-contamination of stored drinking water?|use a clean, covered container|place hands or dirty utensils into the water|leave the container open beside waste|mix it with untreated water|Clean, covered storage helps reduce recontamination.
Which statement about vector control is careful?|The appropriate package depends on the disease and local conditions|One measure guarantees zero risk everywhere|All insects transmit malaria|A net treats contaminated water|Vector-control measures depend on local disease risk and guidance.
Air quality can affect|respiratory and cardiovascular health|only the name of a sport|only the length of a bone|no aspect of health|Air pollution can affect respiratory and cardiovascular systems.
A programme designed without consulting affected people|may miss practical needs and barriers|guarantees equal access|proves all communities have the same priorities|needs no evaluation|Community involvement can reveal needs and barriers that affect implementation.
Good environmental health practice combines|individual actions and maintained public systems|only individual knowledge with no facilities|only construction without operation or maintenance|one action claimed to eliminate every risk|Effective practice often requires both individual behaviour and reliable systems.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
